# Figma-to-Code Workflow

## Step 1: Extract the Design

When given a Figma URL (e.g. `figma.com/design/:fileKey/:fileName?node-id=:nodeId`):

1. Parse the URL to extract `fileKey` and `nodeId` (convert `-` to `:` in nodeId)
2. Call `get_design_context` with `fileKey` and `nodeId` via the Figma MCP
3. Call `get_screenshot` for visual reference
4. If the URL points to a FigJam board, use `get_figjam` instead

Save the screenshot and design context for reference during build.

## Step 2: Build the Component Inventory

Create a table in `docs/design-analysis-[feature].md`:

```markdown
| Element | PF6 Component | Import | Props | Figma Frame | Notes |
|---------|---------------|--------|-------|-------------|-------|
| Main table | Table, Thead, Tr, Th, Td, Tbody | @patternfly/react-table | isExpandable, isSortable | Frame 1 | Expandable rows for CVE details |
| Filter toolbar | Toolbar, ToolbarContent, ToolbarItem | @patternfly/react-core | | Frame 1 | Order: Entity, CVE severity, Fixable |
| Severity badge | Label | @patternfly/react-core | color="red" | Frame 1 | Critical/High/Medium/Low |
```

## Step 3: Verify with PatternFly MCP

For each component in the inventory, use the PatternFly MCP (`user-patternfly-mcp`) to:

1. Confirm the component exists in PF6 (not deprecated from PF5)
2. Check correct prop names and types
3. Find usage examples for complex components (Wizard, Select, MenuToggle)
4. Identify design token names for any colors in the design

Common PF5 to PF6 changes to watch for:
- `Modal` props changed: use `ModalHeader`, `ModalBody`, `ModalFooter` as children
- `Select` replaced with `MenuToggle` + `Select`/`SelectList`/`SelectOption`
- `Chip` replaced with `Label`
- `SimpleList` patterns may differ
- Table requires explicit `Tbody` wrappers around row groups

## Step 4: Check StackRox Upstream

Search `stackrox-upstream/ui/apps/platform/src/` for existing implementations:

1. Find the equivalent page in StackRox (e.g. Vulnerabilities reporting)
2. Note the component structure and data flow
3. Extract GraphQL queries and REST endpoint paths
4. Identify filter patterns and toolbar compositions
5. Document in `docs/reference-patterns-[feature].md`

NEVER edit files in `stackrox-upstream/`.

## Step 5: Build Page by Page

For each page identified in the Figma analysis:

1. Create the page file in `src/pages/` or `src/pages/vN/`
2. Start with layout structure (Page, PageSection, etc.)
3. Add the toolbar with filters in EXACT Figma order (verify against screenshot)
4. Build the data table or content area
5. Add interactive states (dropdowns, modals, wizards)
6. Wire to API layer (mock or real)

## Step 6: Verify Toolbar Order

This is the most common source of iteration. After building:

1. Open the Figma screenshot side-by-side with the running prototype
2. Compare toolbar items left-to-right
3. Compare filter dropdown options and their order
4. Compare table column order
5. Fix any mismatches before moving on

## Step 7: Interactive States Checklist

For each interactive element, verify these states exist:

- [ ] Default / empty state
- [ ] Hover state
- [ ] Active / selected state
- [ ] Disabled state (if applicable)
- [ ] Loading state (if data-dependent)
- [ ] Error state (if applicable)
- [ ] Modal open / closed
- [ ] Wizard step transitions
- [ ] Filter applied / removed
- [ ] Search active / inactive

## Common Patterns

### Filter carry-over (results view to wizard)

```jsx
navigate(`/${routePrefix}/main/vulnerabilities/reports/create`, {
  state: {
    filters: currentFilters,
    savedFilterName: selectedSavedFilter?.name,
    filtersModified: hasUserModifiedFilters,
  }
});
```

### Expandable table rows

```jsx
<Tbody isExpanded={isRowExpanded(row.id)}>
  <Tr>
    <Td expand={{ rowIndex, isExpanded: isRowExpanded(row.id), onToggle: () => toggleRow(row.id) }} />
    {/* data cells */}
  </Tr>
  <Tr isExpanded={isRowExpanded(row.id)}>
    <Td colSpan={columnCount}>
      <ExpandableRowContent>{/* detail content */}</ExpandableRowContent>
    </Td>
  </Tr>
</Tbody>
```

### PF6 Modal

```jsx
<Modal isOpen={isModalOpen} onClose={handleClose} variant="medium">
  <ModalHeader title="Confirm action" />
  <ModalBody>Content here</ModalBody>
  <ModalFooter>
    <Button onClick={handleConfirm}>Confirm</Button>
    <Button variant="link" onClick={handleClose}>Cancel</Button>
  </ModalFooter>
</Modal>
```
